+**Pitfalls and Edge Cases:**
+*   **Floater Artifacts:** Without refraction modeling, NeRF suffers from semi-transparent floaters because sampling points are not aligned with the bent ray path [S1].
+*   **Loss of Refraction Loss:** Removing $L_{ref}$ disrupts fitting because the absence of the correct refracted ray path leads to erroneous sampling points [S1].
+*   **Decoder Substitution:** Substituting the specialized decoder with a simple average leads to significant performance decrease [S1].
+*   **Parameterization:** Using origin and direction instead of 4D light field parameterization worsens accuracy, especially for LPIPS [S1].
+*   **Normal Dependency:** While the student network does not use normals, the teacher does. The method relies on estimating normals from the initial density query [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The method adds the cost of two additional MLP evaluations (Student and Teacher RefNet) and one Decoder MLP evaluation per ray per iteration. The RefNet is shallow (2-3 layers), so the overhead is low compared to the base NeRF [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing parameters for Student RefNet ($2 \times 64 \times 4 + 64$), Teacher RefNet ($3 \times 128 \times 5 + 128$), and Decoder ($2 \times 256 \times 2 + 256$) [S1].
-*   **Measured Performance:** On the Eikonal Fields dataset (Ball, Glass, Pen, WineGlass) and Shiny dataset, RoseNeRF demonstrates state-of-the-art performance without additional information [S1]. It outperforms Ref-NeRF and surpasses NeX (which has 500x more parameters) in detail and achieves competitive specular highlights [S1].
-*   **Strengths:**
-    *   No need for IoR fields or surface masks [S1].
-    *   Simplifies complex refraction to a single event, reducing computational load [S1].
-    *   Avoids floater artifacts common in standard NeRF on refractive objects [S1].
-    *   Easy integration with other NeRF variants [S1].
-*   **Limitations:**
-    *   Assumes a single refraction event; may not handle complex internal reflections or multiple refractions accurately [S1].
-    *   Performance depends on the accuracy of the initial normal estimation [S1].
-    *   Removing the refraction loss disrupts training due to erroneous sampling points [S1].
-    *   Substituting the specialized decoder with a simple computed average leads to a significant decrease in performance [S1].
-    *   Using origin/direction instead of 4D light field worsens accuracy, especially LPIPS [S1].
+*   **Complexity:** The method adds a small MLP (RefNet) and a Decoder MLP to the standard NeRF architecture. The RefNet is shallow (2-3 layers), reducing computational load compared to point-by-point deformation networks [S1].
+*   **Performance:** RoseNeRF demonstrates state-of-the-art performance on the Eikonal Fields’ and Shiny datasets without additional information like IoR [S1]. It outperforms Ref-NeRF and achieves competitive results with NeX (which has 500x more parameters) [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** No need for IoR fields or surface masks. Simplifies complex refraction to a single event. Reduces degrees of freedom.
+*   **Limitations:** Assumes a single refraction event, ignoring multiple internal reflections or refractions. Performance may degrade if the single-refraction assumption is violated significantly [S1].
 
 ## Background
-RoseNeRF (Refracting Once is Enough for NeRF) was introduced in 2024 by Xiaoqian Liang et al. [S1]. It addresses the challenge of novel view synthesis for real-world refractive objects, where standard NeRF fails due to floater artifacts caused by incorrect sampling along bent ray paths [S1]. The method is inspired by Han et al. [20] (Eikonal Fields), who demonstrated the feasibility of reconstructing transparent objects by immersing them in liquid, but RoseNeRF simplifies this by not requiring detailed simulations of light interactions or assumptions about object shapes [S1]. It builds on MS-NeRF [59] for the decoding strategy [S1].
+RoseNeRF was introduced by Xiaoqian Liang et al. in 2024 at the ACM International Conference on Multimedia (ICMR) [S1]. It addresses the challenge of novel view synthesis for refractive objects, a problem where standard NeRF fails due to incorrect sampling along bent light paths [S1]. The method is inspired by Han et al. [20], who reconstructed transparent objects by immersing them in liquid, simplifying the modeling by ignoring complex ray interactions [S1]. RoseNeRF is implemented on top of MS-NeRF [S1].
 
